  <PageHero title="Potala View Hotels" subtitle="Where to stay in Lhasa to wake up to the sight of Potala Palace. Luxury, mid-range, and budget hotels with real window views, foreigner-friendly status checked, and booking tips for 2026." breadcrumb="Hotels" updated="August 2026" />

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-6 py-10 md:py-14">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="luxury">Luxury Hotels with Potala Views</h2>
      <p>These are the rooms you have seen on Instagram. Waking up to Potala Palace glowing in the morning sun through floor-to-ceiling windows — it is one of the great hotel experiences in Asia, and worth every yuan if your budget allows.</p>

      <h3>The St. Regis Lhasa Resort ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</h3>
      <p>The gold standard for Potala views. The Potala View Rooms and Suites face directly east toward the palace, approximately 2 km away across the Lhasa River. At dawn, the palace catches the first light while the valley below is still in shadow. The hotel itself is a destination: gold-leaf ceilings, a stunning indoor pool, and a Tibetan welcome ritual that sets the tone.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Potala view rooms from:</strong> CNY 1,500–2,500/night</li>
        <li><strong>Distance to Potala:</strong> 2 km (5-minute taxi)</li>
        <li><strong>Foreigner-friendly:</strong> Yes</li>
        <li><strong>Booking tip:</strong> You must specifically book a "Potala View" category room. Standard rooms face the mountains, not the palace. Confirm at booking and again at check-in.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Shangri-La Hotel, Lhasa ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</h3>
      <p>Slightly closer to the palace than the St. Regis, with an elevated position that gives its Potala View rooms an unobstructed sightline. The rooftop lounge and restaurant both face the palace, so even if your room does not have the view, you can enjoy it over breakfast or sunset cocktails. The hotel's oxygen lounge is a genuine perk for altitude-struggling guests.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Potala view rooms from:</strong> CNY 1,200–2,000/night</li>
        <li><strong>Distance to Potala:</strong> 1.5 km (20-minute walk)</li>
        <li><strong>Foreigner-friendly:</strong> Yes</li>
        <li><strong>Booking tip:</strong> Request a room on floor 5 or higher. Lower floors may have partially obstructed views.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Four Points by Sheraton Lhasa ⭐⭐⭐⭐</h3>
      <p>The most affordable international chain option with reliable Potala views. The building is positioned directly facing the palace at a favorable angle. Not as luxurious as the St. Regis or Shangri-La, but clean, consistent, and significantly cheaper. A solid choice if you want the view without the five-star price tag.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Potala view rooms from:</strong> CNY 600–900/night</li>
        <li><strong>Distance to Potala:</strong> 1 km (12-minute walk)</li>
        <li><strong>Foreigner-friendly:</strong> Yes</li>
        <li><strong>Booking tip:</strong> Book a "Palace View" room. Some city-view rooms also catch a partial view from higher floors, but do not count on it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="mid-range">Mid-Range Boutique Hotels</h2>
      <p>These are not international chains, but they have the one thing the chains cannot offer: genuine Tibetan character. Rooftop terraces, prayer-flag balconies, and owners who know every tea house in the neighborhood.</p>

      <h3>Tashi Choeta Boutique Hotel</h3>
      <p>The rooftop terrace here is the real draw — an unobstructed, panoramic view of Potala Palace that rivals the luxury hotels at a fraction of the price. Rooms are decorated with Tibetan furniture and textiles. Breakfast is served on the rooftop when weather permits. The Barkhor Street area location puts you in the heart of old Lhasa.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>From:</strong> CNY 300–500/night</li>
        <li><strong>Distance to Potala:</strong> 2.5 km (8-minute taxi)</li>
        <li><strong>Foreigner-friendly:</strong> Yes — confirm at booking</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Yak Hotel</h3>
      <p>A Lhasa backpacker classic that has upgraded over the years. The rooftop restaurant faces Potala directly and is open to non-guests too. Rooms range from basic dorms to comfortable private doubles. The location is excellent — 10 minutes to Barkhor, 20 minutes to Potala on foot. The rooftop is where travelers trade altitude stories over Everest beer.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>From:</strong> CNY 80 (dorm) / CNY 250 (private room)</li>
        <li><strong>Distance to Potala:</strong> 2 km (25-minute walk)</li>
        <li><strong>Foreigner-friendly:</strong> Yes</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Kyichu Hotel</h3>
      <p>A quiet, garden-style hotel set back from the main road. Rooms are arranged around a central courtyard filled with flowers and prayer flags. The Potala view from the upper-floor rooms is more distant but framed beautifully by the courtyard trees. Excellent value for the atmosphere alone.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>From:</strong> CNY 250–400/night</li>
        <li><strong>Distance to Potala:</strong> 2 km (8-minute taxi)</li>
        <li><strong>Foreigner-friendly:</strong> Yes</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="budget">Budget and Hostel Options</h2>
      <p>You will not get a Potala view from your pillow at these prices, but you will get a rooftop, a social atmosphere, and a bed in the world's highest city.</p>

      <h3>Lhasa Pingcuo Kangsang Hostel</h3>
      <p>The most social hostel in Lhasa. Dorm beds, private rooms, a lively common area, and a rooftop with distant Potala views. Excellent for solo travelers looking to meet people. The staff can help arrange shared taxis to nearby attractions.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>From:</strong> CNY 50 (dorm) / CNY 180 (private room)</li>
        <li><strong>Foreigner-friendly:</strong> Yes</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Fengma Feiyang Hostel</h3>
      <p>Smaller and quieter than Pingcuo Kangsang. The rooftop directly faces Potala, making it a budget traveler's secret for sunset photography without paying for a luxury hotel bar. Clean, friendly, and well-run.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>From:</strong> CNY 60 (dorm) / CNY 200 (private room)</li>
        <li><strong>Foreigner-friendly:</strong> Yes — confirm at booking</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="where-to-stay">Which Neighborhood Should You Choose?</h2>

      <h3>Barkhor / Old Town</h3>
      <p>The spiritual and sensory heart of Lhasa. Stay here if you want to walk out your door into the Barkhor circumambulation, be surrounded by pilgrims and prayer wheels, and have Jokhang Temple a 2-minute walk away. Narrow alleys, rooftop tea houses, and the sound of chanting. Downsides: older buildings, variable room quality, and no direct vehicle access to many hotels (you walk the last 100–300 meters).</p>

      <h3>Beijing Road / City Center</h3>
      <p>The practical choice. Closer to Potala Palace, wider streets, easier taxi access, and more consistent hotel quality. The chains are here. You trade atmosphere for convenience. This is the right choice if you have limited mobility, are traveling with young children, or simply want a predictable hotel experience at altitude.</p>

      <h3>Lhasa River / Southern District</h3>
      <p>Where the luxury resorts sit. Quieter, greener, and with the best Potala panoramas. You will need taxis to get anywhere (CNY 10–20 to most attractions), but the views and serenity are unmatched. Best for couples, photographers, and anyone who wants to decompress between sightseeing sessions.</p>

      <h2 id="booking-tips">Booking Tips for Foreign Travelers</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Always confirm foreigner status.</strong> Before booking on any platform, contact the hotel directly and ask: "Do you accept foreign guests?" (你们接待外宾吗?). If the answer is no, do not book — you will be refused at check-in, and Chinese platforms may not refund you.</li>
        <li><strong>Use Trip.com (formerly Ctrip).</strong> It is the most foreigner-friendly Chinese booking platform. English interface, accepts international credit cards, and customer service can mediate if there are hotel issues.</li>
        <li><strong>Booking.com and Agoda</strong> list Lhasa hotels, but inventory is limited. Use them for price comparison, but verify foreigner-friendliness independently.</li>
        <li><strong>Book early for peak season.</strong> July–August and the first week of October (National Day holiday) see occupancy rates above 90% at all price levels. Book at least 3–4 weeks ahead.</li>
        <li><strong>Check for oxygen.</strong> Many mid-range and luxury hotels offer in-room oxygen systems or oxygen lounges. At 3,650 meters, this is not a gimmick — it can be the difference between a good night's sleep and a miserable one. Prioritize hotels with oxygen if it is your first time at altitude.</li>
        <li><strong>Printed reservation confirmations.</strong> Have a printed copy of your hotel booking. Some hotels in Tibet require it for registration with local authorities, and Wi-Fi is not always reliable enough to pull up an email.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
